    "education": "<h1>The classic three-clause for loop</h1>\n<h2>The idea</h2>\n<p><code>for init; cond; post</code> is Go's only loop keyword; it covers counting up, down, and while-style conditions.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Explicit counters are needed whenever you build or transform by index rather than by range.</p>\n<h2>Watch out</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Off-by-one: use <code>i &gt;= 1</code> to include 1 and stop before 0.</li>\n<li>Return a non-nil empty slice for <code>n &lt;= 0</code> (len 0 is enough here).</li>\n</ul>",
