    "description": "<div class=\"p-head\"><h1 class=\"p-title\">Reverse via Index Loop</h1><div class=\"p-meta\"><span class=\"pill lv-junior\">junior</span></div></div><h2>Context</h2>\n<p>Walking an index from the end builds a reversed copy without touching the input.</p>\n<h2>Task</h2>\n<p>Implement <code>Reverse</code> in reverseloop.go returning a new slice.</p>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> change the function signature or the tests.</p>\n<h2>Examples</h2>\n<pre class=\"md\"><code>\nReverse([]int{1,2,3})  // =&gt; [3 2 1]\nReverse(nil)           // =&gt; []\nReverse([]int{9})      // =&gt; [9]\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"topics-foot\"><summary class=\"topics-lbl\">Topics <span class=\"topics-n\">3</span></summary><div class=\"tchips\"><span class=\"tchip\">Descending index loop</span><span class=\"tchip\">Build new slice</span><span class=\"tchip\">Immutability</span></div></details>",
    "education": "<h1>Index-based reversal</h1>\n<h2>The idea</h2>\n<p>Reading source back-to-front into a new slice keeps the original intact \u2014 a copy, not an in-place swap.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Non-destructive transforms are safer defaults when the caller may still need the input.</p>\n<h2>Watch out</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Reversing in place would mutate the caller's slice (shared backing array).</li>\n<li>Start the index at <code>len(xs)-1</code>, end at <code>&gt;= 0</code>.</li>\n</ul>",
