    "description": "<div class=\"p-head\"><h1 class=\"p-title\">Sum with For-Range</h1><div class=\"p-meta\"><span class=\"pill lv-junior\">junior</span></div></div><h2>Context</h2>\n<p><code>for range</code> walks a slice yielding index and value; ignore the index with <code>_</code></p>\n<p>when only the value matters.</p>\n<h2>Task</h2>\n<p>Implement <code>SumRange</code> in sumrange.go using a for-range loop.</p>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> change the function signature or the tests.</p>\n<h2>Examples</h2>\n<pre class=\"md\"><code>\nSumRange(nil)             // =&gt; 0\nSumRange([]int{1,2,3,4})  // =&gt; 10\nSumRange([]int{-1, 1})    // =&gt; 0\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"topics-foot\"><summary class=\"topics-lbl\">Topics <span class=\"topics-n\">3</span></summary><div class=\"tchips\"><span class=\"tchip\">for-range</span><span class=\"tchip\">Blank identifier</span><span class=\"tchip\">Accumulator</span></div></details>",
    "education": "<h1>The for-range loop</h1>\n<h2>The idea</h2>\n<p><code>range</code> over a slice returns <code>(index, value)</code> copies; discarding the index with <code>_</code> gives a clean value iteration.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>It's the default way to traverse slices, maps, strings, and channels in Go.</p>\n<h2>Watch out</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>v</code> is a copy; writing to it does not change the slice.</li>\n<li>A nil slice ranges zero times \u2014 no special case needed.</li>\n</ul>",
