    "id": "01-language-basics/04-functions/middle/zipwith",
    "title": "Zip With",
    "level": "middle",
    "tag": "backend",
    "description": "<div class=\"p-head\"><h1 class=\"p-title\">Zip With</h1><div class=\"p-meta\"><span class=\"pill lv-middle\">middle</span></div></div><h2>Context</h2>\n<p>Zipping walks two slices in lockstep, stopping at the shorter one, applying a</p>\n<p>binary function to each pair.</p>\n<h2>Task</h2>\n<p>Implement <code>ZipWith</code> in zipwith.go.</p>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> change the function signature or the tests.</p>\n<h2>Examples</h2>\n<pre class=\"md\"><code>\nZipWith([]int{1,2,3}, []int{10,20}, add) // =&gt; [11 22]\nZipWith(nil, []int{1}, f)                // =&gt; []\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"topics-foot\"><summary class=\"topics-lbl\">Topics <span class=\"topics-n\">3</span></summary><div class=\"tchips\"><span class=\"tchip\">Lockstep iteration</span><span class=\"tchip\">Binary combiner</span><span class=\"tchip\">Shorter wins</span></div></details>",
    "education": "<h1>Element-wise combination of two sequences</h1>\n<h2>The idea</h2>\n<p>ZipWith iterates two inputs together, bounded by the shorter, applying a two-argument function.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters</h2>\n<p>Vector math, merging parallel columns, and paired transforms use it.</p>\n<h2>Watch out</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stop at the shorter length; indexing past it panics.</li>\n<li>Result length equals the shorter input's length.</li>\n</ul>",
    "starter": "// Package zipwith \u2014 Gopher Workplace challenge.\npackage zipwith\n\n// ZipWith combines a and b elementwise with f, up to the shorter length.\nfunc ZipWith(a, b []int, f func(int, int) int) []int {\n\t// TODO(candidate): implement this from scratch so all tests pass.\n\tpanic(\"not implemented\")\n}\n",
